cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software, please cite it as below."
title: "entities-assembly: the branch assembly that builds the multiplayer-fabric Godot fork"
abstract: >
  This repository holds no engine code. It holds the recipe that assembles the
  multiplayer-fabric Godot fork from upstream feature branches and pushes the result
  to entities-godot: a gitassembly file naming each branch and how it is merged, and
  an Elixir driver that runs the assembler and force-pushes the assembled branches
  and a CalVer tag. A merge order is a decision, and keeping it in a file makes it
  one that can be read and argued with rather than one held in somebody's shell
  history.
authors:
  - family-names: Lee
    given-names: "K. S. Ernest (iFire)"
    email: ernest.lee@chibifire.com
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0003-4570-1436"
version: 0.1.0-dev.1
date-released: "2026-08-17"
license: MIT
repository-code: "https://github.com/v-sekai-multiplayer-fabric/entities-assembly"
keywords:
  - godot
  - branch-management
  - build-automation
references:

  - type: software
    title: "git-assembler: assemble git branches automatically"
    url: "https://gitlab.com/wavexx/git-assembler"
    notes: >
      The program this repository's assembler replaces. It read the gitassembly file and performed
      the stage and merge steps; this repository supplies the file and the driver.

  - type: software
    title: "entities-godot: the multiplayer-fabric Godot fork"
    url: "https://github.com/v-sekai-multiplayer-fabric/entities-godot"
    notes: >
      The output. The assembled multiplayer-fabric-base and multiplayer-fabric branches
      are force-pushed there, so that repository's history is a product of this file
      rather than of a sequence of merges anybody performed by hand.

  - type: software
    title: "Godot Engine"
    authors:
      - name: "Godot Engine contributors"
    url: "https://godotengine.org/"
    notes: "The upstream every assembled branch is a feature branch of."
